1:# ZFC and virtual-class proof checker
12:The command reader runs until EOF with `tapeEnd`; it also exits on quit or a fatal error. There is no fixed command-count limit. `tapeEnd` binds an `int8` byte, and body reads advance the same tape. The explicit, pure substitution traversal has its own finite work budget; exhausting it is a fatal error, never permission to accept a partial substitution.
16:Before reading input, the checker prints `SEED\n` and flushes. Send two unsigned 64-bit decimal words `k0 k1\n`. The checker installs them as the 128-bit SipHash key and prints `READY\n`, flushed before commands are read. Missing, malformed or overflowing seed words produce `ERR\n` and terminate. For example:
32:All input is token based, with space, tab, CR and LF separators. Commands may share a line, and a final token may end at EOF. Integers must be decimal values from 0 through `2^64-1`; signs and other characters are rejected. Names are nonempty byte tokens of at most 1024 bytes. A missing argument, malformed integer, oversized name, unsupported opcode, checked arithmetic overflow, or traversal exhaustion produces `ERR\n` and terminates. Invalid IDs, wrong object types, duplicate names and failed proof checks produce `NO\n`; the checker continues.
59:| `23 proofNode name`                   | Name a schema certificate                                                         |
88:A named schema is a proof-certificate template, not an unchecked theorem assertion. Each application expands its class arguments and rechecks every reachable axiom/inference node. Ill-typed nodes cannot be constructed, and an invalid MP template is rejected when instantiated. Only the checked root theorem is appended; intermediate proof nodes never enter public history. A failed instance leaves theorem history and names unchanged, although private formula/class allocations may remain. Wrong argument counts and bad class IDs consume the declared arguments and return `NO`, preserving token synchronization.
96:The actual IIL hash is checked against the published SipHash 24-byte test vector and 66 additional independent oracle cases. The Rocq build checks the published vector for the actual generated hash procedure. [SipHash.v](imperative/proof/SipHash.v) additionally proves the general execution refinement for every seed and tuple: termination, the full digest, the bucket index and its bounds, preservation of every other array and metadata cell, and unchanged input/output. [HashScan.v](imperative/proof/HashScan.v) proves the generated collision-chain scan terminates, returns only exact tuple matches, and finds every reachable match within its iteration budget under descending-link bounds. [InternTail.v](imperative/proof/InternTail.v) proves the actual generated insertion continuation, including overflow rejection, preserved earlier IDs and descending links. [HashTable.v](imperative/proof/HashTable.v) proves bucket reachability and uniqueness survive insertion, a scan miss implies freshness, and canonical lookup returns the original ID. [Rehash.v](imperative/proof/Rehash.v) proves the generated rehash iteration executes SipHash and preserves processed-entry reachability. [Rebuild.v](imperative/proof/Rebuild.v) composes the iterations into the complete generated rebuilding loop, proving termination, preservation of earlier formula IDs and the processed-entry invariant. [Clear.v](imperative/proof/Clear.v) and [RehashFull.v](imperative/proof/RehashFull.v) prove rehash preparation, the complete rehash procedure and overflow rejection. [Intern.v](imperative/proof/Intern.v) proves the actual generated interner terminates and either raises its fatal flag or returns the canonical tuple ID, preserving earlier IDs and logical state. [Representation.v](imperative/proof/Representation.v) connects the arena to ZFC formulas, preserving semantic uniqueness and every earlier formula meaning. [Bootstrap.v](imperative/proof/Bootstrap.v) establishes the seeded empty arena and native falsity allocation at ID zero. These proofs do not assume that hashing is injective. [Observation.v](imperative/proof/Observation.v) proves that internal helpers do not flush, including substitution and schema evaluation. [TokenByte.v](imperative/proof/TokenByte.v), [ReadToken.v](imperative/proof/ReadToken.v) and [ReadNat.v](imperative/proof/ReadNat.v) prove byte accumulation and canonical token/integer reads, including the pending first command byte, exact unread input, and checked arithmetic. [DecimalInput.v](imperative/proof/DecimalInput.v) connects the protocol decimal encoder to the actual generated reader: every 64-bit wire integer is read back unchanged, with no additional flush observations. [SeedInput.v](imperative/proof/SeedInput.v) refines the actual `main` prefix: the exact `SEED` prompt flush occurs before reading; both decimal seed words are installed unchanged; the logical state, command input suffix and existing flush trace are preserved. [SeedBootstrap.v](imperative/proof/SeedBootstrap.v) connects that parsed state to falsity allocation. [Startup.v](imperative/proof/Startup.v) proves a complete execution of the actual `main` when EOF follows the seed words, including both exact flush snapshots, full input consumption, termination and the canonical falsity arena. [QuitDialogue.v](imperative/proof/QuitDialogue.v) proves the actual `main` on a seed exchange followed by quit, including the exact three flush snapshots, stop flag, preserved formula arena and arbitrary later bytes left unread. General dialogues and opcodes 1 through 26 remain open. Native tests cover collisions, repeated rehashing, immutable IDs, names, schema typing, capture avoidance, failed instances, seed failures, EOF and interactive flushing.
